### 3.4 DiD Estimators for the ATT and ATN

Here, we extend existing doubly robust estimators for the ATT under SUTVA in order to estimate both the ATT and ATN under our modified SUTVA with a binary exposure mapping, hi​th\_{it}. To do so, we denote RiR\_{i} as a binary indicator representing assignment to the exposure group of interest for the particular estimand and Zi​tr=t​RiZ^{r}\_{it}=tR\_{i} as an indicator representing the status of this relevant exposure at time tt. Specifically, Ri=1R\_{i}=1 refers to the treatment group in the ATT comparison and the neighboring control group in the ATN comparison, whereas Ri=0R\_{i}=0 refers to the control group in both. We focus on methods for longitudinal panel data, corresponding to our data example. However, these methods can be readily adapted to the setting where pre- and post-tax data are collected on different populations, i.e. cross-sectional data ([Sant’Anna and Zhao (2020)](https://arxiv.org/html/2301.06697v2#bib.bib16)).

